WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
20 years ago, people had hobbies. Now,

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:07.880
they have a screen. The average person

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.200
now spends 70 hours a week staring at a

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:13.040
screen. That's not just wasting time,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:16.440
it's slowly destroying our brain. I went

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.960
from a homeless teen to an MIT grad to

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.920
building and advising companies worth

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.040
billions. Somewhere in that journey, I

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.720
almost lost every hobby I ever loved.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.520
But, I also saw a pattern. The most

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:32.880
successful people fiercely protect their

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.720
seemingly useless hobbies because that's

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.840
their best defense against brain rot. In

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.160
this video, I'll show you why hobbies

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.400
matter more than ever, how they retrain

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.320
your brain for success and fulfillment,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.080
and share a four-part framework for

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.160
choosing hobbies that can help you

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.760
change your life and your career. The

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.160
first thing we need to understand is

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.560
that brain rot isn't what you think it

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.000
is. Our brains are under a two-stage

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.680
attack. Research from Nielsen reported

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.520
that Americans spend 70 hours a week

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.080
consuming media across different

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.360
screens. That's about 10 hours a day.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.560
Our screens have become our full-time

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:12.280
employers, and they pay us in brain rot.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.400
We're all facing the same dangerous

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.160
challenge. And that's because the social

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.680
media we consume is programmed to give

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:21.760
you rapid dopamine hit. Now, there are

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.840
three side effects of social media.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.280
First, our brain starts craving more of

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.040
those hits. So, we start needing more

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.680
hits for the same mental rewards. Second

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:35.560
side effect, we start focusing more on

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:38.080
sharing versus caring. [music] You go to

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.600
any concert nowadays or watch the sunset

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.440
on a beach, and you'll see people around

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.840
you watching it through their phones.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.800
We're not enjoying our experiences,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.160
we're just camera operators for our

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.360
followers. And this leads to the most

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:55.600
depressing third side effect, anhedonia.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:58.720
It's a clinical term about our inability

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.600
to feel pleasure from normal things. But

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.200
that is only the first stage attack.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.160
There [music] is an equally alarming

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:09.880
second stage attack happening. AI.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.920
Social media changes our attention. AI

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.320
changes our agency. Microsoft [music]

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.160
just studied over 300 professionals and

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.840
found something kind of terrifying. When

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.760
people are overconfident in AI, they

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.880
basically turn off their own brains.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.600
They stop double-checking their work and

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.440
their critical thinking just [music]

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.120
shuts down. It's like you're sending

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.160
someone else to the gym and then

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.360
wondering why your own health isn't

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.320
improving. AI doesn't outsource your

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.959
tasks, it outsources your mind, your

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:42.680
agency. And in this world of

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:44.959
hyperstimulated junk food, hobbies are

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.560
the nutritional meal. So why are your

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:50.200
hobbies so important to your brain? I

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.840
recently recorded a meeting with my team

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:55.280
and I was listening to the playback and

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:57.920
I was just horrified. My thoughts were

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.360
jumping around mid-sentence, rambling, I

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.320
had no coherence. I realized that my

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.560
brain had become a mirror of my TikTok

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:08.480
feed, totally fragmented. Sitting there

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.840
with my headphone on, I literally yelled

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.840
at myself and said, "Come on, stop

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.360
babbling, get to the actual question."

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.840
That was a wake-up call. If you don't

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.720
keep training for focus, you're going to

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.560
lose it. Your brain doesn't adapt or

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.760
grow when you're comfortable. It adapts

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:28.760
when reality surprises you. No surprise,

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.720
no change. So when you're scrolling your

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:34.239
feed like a zombie or let AI write your

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.880
essay or your strategy document, there

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:38.640
is no struggle. There is no surprise.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.959
There is no discovery.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.800
No upgrade.

00:03:39.959 --> 00:03:45.160
I call it deepfake mastery. It looks

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.600
great, but it's not real. You know, in

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:51.120
medicine, if you put an arm in a cast

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:53.160
for 6 months, the muscle will atrophy.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.640
It'll literally degenerate, It'll eat

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:58.120
itself. And then, when the cast comes

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:00.760
off, you're going to need rehab to even

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.120
open your wrists. Hobbies are your

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.160
rehab. When you're cooking, you'll make

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.440
a mess. You'll play an instrument,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:10.600
you'll miss a note. A hobby allows you

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.959
to struggle, to be surprised. It forces

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:15.160
your brain to [music] upgrade. Now, if

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:17.040
you're ambitious and you're successful

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:20.037
or you're trying to be successful, you

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.640
probably going to feel guilty about your

00:04:20.037 --> 00:04:24.760
[music] time spent on hobbies. Our 24/7

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.400
culture tells us that hobbies are

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:30.040
selfish, that they're extra, that time

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.200
you spend working on yourself is time

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.480
stolen from work [music] or from the

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.758
people who need you. Nothing could be

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:35.760
further than the truth.

00:04:35.758 --> 00:04:38.000
>> [music]

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.880
>> And the proof comes from an unlikely

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.440
place. Researchers at Michigan State

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.800
spent 20 years studying 773

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:49.840
Nobel Prize winners. What they found

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.120
was, well, surprising. Nobel Prize

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.040
winners had three times more serious

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.400
hobbies than their peers. And they were

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:58.760
nine times more likely to have formal

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.919
training in crafts [music] or fine arts

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:05.320
or music. For the world's most capable

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:08.280
minds, hobbies weren't a guilty break

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:10.320
from their work. It fueled their work.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:12.280
But then the question is, how do you

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:14.360
pick the right hobbies? For that, you

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:17.440
need a framework. And we're going to use

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:20.298
the VIBE framework. Every thriving,

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.800
high-performing individual needs these

00:05:20.298 --> 00:05:24.560
[music] four pillars. Vitality.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.400
Are you running on empty? Then pick a

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.200
hobby that gets your heart rate up.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.760
Dance class, Pilates, [music]

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.080
tracking, martial arts, climbing,

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:37.280
pickleball, all fit here. Inquiry. Are

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.960
you easily bored? Then pick a hobby that

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:42.160
forces you to be a beginner again. Learn

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.240
a new language, play chess, take a

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.480
course. Belonging.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:50.040
Do you have a real community or just a

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.960
list of contacts. Try a hobby that

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:55.600
weaves you into [music] a tribe, a

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:58.040
running club or a band, a non-profit, a

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.960
local book club, coaching little kids.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.520
And finally, the fourth pillar,

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:05.160
expression. Do you consume more than

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.840
create? Then try a hobby that pulls

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.360
something from inside of you and puts it

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.400
out into the world. [music] Photography,

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:15.000
painting, playing an instrument,

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:17.080
pottery, writing, cooking. This one can

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.160
be a long list. So, whether your guilt

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:22.160
is I should be working all the time or I

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.960
should be taking care of someone else,

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.240
this is your answer. Work [music] on

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:28.640
yourself first. And one tiny observation

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:31.200
about this, your hobbies don't have to

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:34.000
fit neatly into one of those four

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.919
quadrants. Painting, for example, can be

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.760
an inquiry and an expression. For me,

00:06:36.919 --> 00:06:40.760
when I used to play in the band, it used

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.919
to hit all four quadrants for me

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:45.440
simultaneously. So, what's the action

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:48.160
item? You don't have to overthink it.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:50.120
Pick one or two hobbies that you love,

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.640
apply the framework to see where they

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.040
fit, if they fit, and then follow the

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:57.480
rule of three.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.800
Do it three times and see if you want to

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.880
commit to it. If it's not a good fit,

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:04.240
pick something else. Make sure you pick

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:07.120
a hobby you'll actually do with full

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.440
attention because you don't pick a hobby

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.760
just to escape, [music]

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:12.640
you choose it to come back to yourself.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:15.240
Now, what's the best way to keep a hobby

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.240
going? Focus on play, not on

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.240
performance. This is the fastest way to

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:22.720
kill a hobby. Hobbies are your rehab.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:26.040
Don't turn rehab into a performance

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:27.600
review. The moment your hobby becomes a

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.240
scoreboard,

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.320
it becomes a grind that won't restore

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.440
you. The fastest way to kill a hobby is

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.200
to post [clears throat] about it. The

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.600
second you bring an audience into the

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.400
room, you stop playing for yourself and

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.000
you start performing for them. You're

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:43.600
literally outsourcing your joy to the

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.080
algorithm. You know, it's like going to

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:48.640
Paris and spending the whole time taking

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.440
a selfie with the portrait of Mona Lisa,

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.480
so you can show the world you were

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:55.120
there. And you're still trapped behind

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:58.200
the same screen. So, the key question, I

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:00.920
think, is who this hobby is for. If it

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:01.920
is for you, you'll build. If it is for

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:04.080
them,

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.120
you'll judge. If your hobby feels like a

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.120
performance review, then you've lost the

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.320
plot. So, how do you actually protect

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.280
the play?

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:13.919
Not by fighting the urge to go back to

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:17.280
doom scrolling. Social media is like an

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:19.360
endless river. You don't stop a river by

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.160
standing in front of it. You cut a new

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.000
channel and let the water find its way.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.680
Here's how you build that channel for

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.240
yourself. When you're enjoying your

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:31.280
hobby, don't shoot it and don't post it.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:33.400
Focus on minutes, not on metrics. Start

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.039
with cheap gear, don't overspend. And

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.960
finally, after each session,

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:39.960
just ask one question.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.960
Did I feel more alive

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:46.520
or more judged? Your hobby is where you

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:49.240
go to be a messy, imperfect human in a

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:52.280
world that demands you to be polished

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:54.440
and optimized machine. So, yes, hobbies

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:57.280
is how you come back to yourself. And

00:08:54.440 --> 00:09:00.920
it's supposed to restore you, not rank

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:03.400
you. There's no win or lose,

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.400
only the play. And here's what we learn

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:08.520
from the happiest people in the world.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:11.520
In the World Happiness Report in 2025,

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:15.000
Finland ranked number one for the eighth

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:17.720
time in a row, and US fell to number 23,

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.800
its lowest ever. Now, Finland is no

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:23.080
utopia. It endures some of the longest,

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.720
toughest, [music] bleakest winters

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:27.880
anywhere in the world. But their

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.560
environment is quietly built to reduce

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:32.839
stress and increase real-world

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.720
experiences. First, they're surrounded

00:09:32.839 --> 00:09:38.680
by nature, and it's a culture of walking

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:41.360
and biking [music] and hiking. Vitality,

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:43.600
check. Second, university education is

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.840
publicly supported and affordable.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.760
Inquiry, check. And in that society,

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.920
there is higher trust, [music]

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:53.436
stronger sense of community, and lower

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:56.080
inequality. So, belonging, check.

00:09:53.436 --> 00:09:59.000
[music] And culturally, they care less

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:01.480
about constant competition or signaling

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:04.800
higher status [music] and success. They

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:07.480
create because they want to. Expression,

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:09.360
check. So, you see, Finland has actually

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.560
built their [music] culture around the

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.080
VIBE framework. And the people there are

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.680
the happiest on Earth. The lesson is

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.360
that the life you build outside of work

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.480
should not be seen as a distraction.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.040
It's the most [music] irreplaceable gift

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.840
you can give yourself

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.560
and to everyone around you. You know,

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:30.480
machines and AI can replicate your

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:32.280
output, but they can never replicate the

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.360
life that produced it. I've been

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:37.120
thinking about this, that the more

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.920
machines become like humans, the less

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:40.720
we'll have to be like machines. And

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:43.880
hobbies [music] connect you to your

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:47.320
inner human being. There is a parable

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:49.800
that I love. A traveler sees three stone

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:52.200
masons working very hard in the

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:55.440
sweltering heat, and they're cutting

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:58.040
blocks of stones. So, he's wondering, he

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.520
asked them what they're doing. The first

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:02.480
one says, "Sir, can't you see?

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:04.720
I'm cutting stones."

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.120
The traveler turns to the second one.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:09.600
The second one says, "Well, [music]

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.680
I'm earning a wage to provide for my

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:12.560
family."

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:16.080
But the third one stands [music] up

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:18.640
tall, looks up, and says, "I am building

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:21.080
a cathedral."

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:24.280
Same work, different meaning. Your

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:28.412
hobbies don't owe you productivity or

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:28.680
followers or wages. They owe you joy.

00:11:28.412 --> 00:11:31.480
>> [music]

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:34.360
>> They bring meaning to your moments

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:36.272
because your life is not just a pile of

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.560
rocks stacked higher and higher.

00:11:36.272 --> 00:11:39.040
>> [music]

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:41.320
>> It is a cathedral.

